INT. VICTORIAN ICE HOUSE - ESTATE GROUNDS - EVENING (1887)

A brick dome half-sunk in the earth. Blocks of ice stacked in straw, sawdust floor, a sharp cold that smells of wet stone. A lantern hangs from a hook. Breath clouds in the light.

ABIGAIL FENWICK-HALE, 30s, housekeeper, shawl over her shoulders, counts blocks with a slate. TOBIAS RUSK, 50s, groundskeeper, canvas coat, sits on a crate, whittling. A STRANGER, 40s, in a good coat and muddy boots, stands near the entrance holding a bowler hat.

ABIGAIL
(to both)
Forty-two blocks. Enough for the garden party.

TOBIAS
Forty-two's plenty.

ABIGAIL
You'll want to turn the straw before the guests arrive.

TOBIAS
I'll do it, Miss Abigail.

The Stranger clears his throat.

STRANGER
Forgive me. I'm looking for the master of the house.

TOBIAS
He's up at the manor, sir.

STRANGER
I tried the door. Nobody answered.

ABIGAIL
The door is closed after four.

STRANGER
So I discovered.

ABIGAIL
And you are?

STRANGER
A friend of the family.

TOBIAS
(under his breath)
Never seen him.

ABIGAIL
Which family?

STRANGER
The Ashworths.

Tobias stops whittling. The shavings fall.

ABIGAIL
The Ashworths left in March.

STRANGER
Did they? I hadn't heard. I'm afraid I've been abroad.

ABIGAIL
Where abroad?

STRANGER
Calcutta.

ABIGAIL
That's a long way to come for an empty house.

STRANGER
I came for the cold.

ABIGAIL
Pardon?

STRANGER
The ice house. They kept something here, once. A sealed tin.

TOBIAS
Nothing's here but ice, sir.

STRANGER
Of course. I only wished to be certain.

He turns his hat in his hands. Raises his eyes.

STRANGER (CONT'D)
My name is Rupert Ashworth. I was supposed to have inherited.

Abigail's slate slips from her fingers and clatters on the floor. Tobias rises, knife in hand.

ABIGAIL
Mr. Rupert died in the Punjab. We had the letter.

RUPERT
Reports of my death were premature.

TOBIAS
(to Abigail)
Miss, I'd stand behind me.

She doesn't move. The lantern flickers.

RUPERT
I only want the tin.

ABIGAIL
(to the cold air)
God help us.

RUPERT
I'd settle for that, too.

He smiles. A thin, apologetic, tired smile. Behind him, the ice house door creaks in the wind, and all three turn to look at it.
